### Structure Rules

- Minimal, clean structure — no unnecessary nesting.
- Every Python package directory MUST have `__init__.py`.
- ALWAYS include the language's dependency manifest (`requirements.txt`, `pyproject.toml`, `package.json`, `go.mod`, `Cargo.toml`, etc.).
- ALWAYS include the primary entry point (`main.py`, `app.py`, `main.go`, `main.ts`, `index.ts`, etc.).
- For browser/frontend projects: ALWAYS include `index.html` and the entry script that bootstraps the app.
- ALWAYS include every tooling config file the stack needs to build and run (`tsconfig.json`, `vite.config.ts`, `jest.config.js`, ...). These are NOT optional.
- ALWAYS include a `tests/` directory with a unit-test file per source module plus at least one integration test.
- Every module you plan must have a consumer: some other file's purpose line must say it uses it. No orphan modules.
- Do NOT include `README.md`, `LICENSE`, `.gitignore`.
- Include `Dockerfile` ONLY if the project genuinely requires containerization.

### Required JSON Output

When you have enough context, return ONE raw JSON object (no fences, no prose) with exactly three keys:

- **`software_blueprint_details`** — `name`, `version`, `summary` (2–3 sentences), `key_features` (list of strings).
- **`folder_structure`** — raw ASCII directory tree of the full repository.
- **`files`** — dict mapping every file path in the tree to a single sentence stating what the file does and which other files consume it (e.g. `"src/storage.py": "Atomic JSON persistence layer with corrupt-file recovery, used by src/engine.py"`).

The `files` keys must match the tree exactly — same paths, nothing missing, nothing extra.
